// fail.dot -- Self-test fixture for the CI-correctness self-test workflow.
//
// Deliberately, deterministically converges to a FAIL terminal: a single
// tool_command= node that always exits 1, with NO outgoing edge that
// matches a "fail" outcome. Per the engine's edge-selection contract, a
// plain edge is an implicit success gate -- it never fires for a FAIL
// outcome -- so the engine finds no matching edge from this node and hard
// -fails the whole pipeline (status: "fail", error_type: "no_matching_edge").
//
// No LLM node anywhere in this graph -- it needs no API key, and the
// outcome is deterministic on every run (a shell "exit 1", not a model's
// judgment call).
//
// What running this proves (see .github/workflows/attractor-self-test.yml):
//   - Before PR #19, _run_attractor() returned exit code 0 whenever
//     session.execute() didn't raise -- a FAIL pipeline like this one
//     produced a GREEN check mark, indistinguishable from pass.dot.
//   - After PR #19, _exit_code_for_attractor_result() inspects the
//     pipeline's terminal JSON status and maps a non-success status to
//     process exit code 1.
//
// The workflow runs this fixture with continue-on-error: true (a FAIL
// pipeline is the EXPECTED, correct result here) and then asserts the
// step's outcome was "failure" -- i.e. the action process actually exited
// non-zero. If that assertion doesn't fire, Defect 1 has regressed.
digraph SelfTestFail {
    graph [goal="amplifier-app-actions self-test: pipeline that must fail"]

    start [shape=Mdiamond, label="Start"]

    boom [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Always fails",
        tool_command="echo 'intentional self-test failure'; exit 1"
    ]

    done [shape=Msquare, label="Done"]

    // No condition="outcome=fail" edge from boom, and the plain edge below
    // only matches on success -- so a FAIL outcome from boom has no
    // matching outgoing edge, and the engine hard-fails the pipeline.
    start -> boom -> done
}
